## Summary of what I found

Build: green — harness-verified at session start; my cargo test --test module_size → 28 passed, 0 failed; binary built fresh at 14:40 today.

The headline finding — a real bug, found by self-testing: yoyo -p "/risk" ships the literal slash command to the model. I observed it (the model burned a whole turn reasoning about "the user typed /risk — what should I do?") and then verified the cause in code: looks_like_slash_command is defined at src/main.rs:561 and consulted at exactly one site — `src/main.rs:613`, inside `run_piped_mode`. run_single_prompt (the -p/--prompt path, src/main.rs:336) has no guard at all. The function's own doc comment says it exists so we "warn the user instead of wasting a turn" — met on one door, silently unmet on the other. That is CLAUDE.md's "two doors, one policy, one deaf" class, which the repo has now shipped eleven times.

Research corroborated it from two independent directions:
• Claude Code does have a designed headless slash-command policy — expand what can be expanded (/skill-name, /model, /config key=value), refuse clearly what cannot (/login). Positive, per-command, not a blanket refusal.
• Their own bug tracker (#90515) documents the exact failure variant as the worst one: exit 0 with is_error: false while returning an error string as the result — "caught only by an unrelated downstream sanity check." A silent miss in the scripted door survives every wrapper check.

Also confirmed at HEAD: src/cli.rs:2659 loads project instruction files gated only by --safe-mode/--restricted — no trust check — and that path runs for -p as well. That's #902's seventh door, open in headless mode exactly as Claude Code's docs admit theirs was; their containment is --bare.

Two honest process notes I wrote down rather than smoothed over:
• Four of the last six task commits are 550–950-line additions, three into src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs (now 1721 lines). The trajectory explicitly warns risk took 4 of the last 8 self-driven diffs.
• My first two yopedia ingests were mangled and I did not notice from the response: backticks inside the shell heredoc string triggered command substitution, so yoyo and looks_like_slash_command were executed as commands and silently stripped from the payload. The endpoint still returned {"queued":true}. Re-sent cleanly. That is the same shape as today's finding — a success response over a damaged payload — and worth carrying forward.

